\]
with \(h=1\), the reduced LP has
\[
S=(0.2,3,4.8),\qquad B=(1,9,2),
\]
and certified value
\[
z=291/100.
\]
One primal certificate is
\[
x_{0,0.4}=1,\qquad x_{1,0.3}=0.3,\qquad
x_{1,0.4}=1.5,\qquad x_{2,0.4}=2.
\]
The dual
\[
\alpha_1=0,\qquad \alpha_2=1/4,\qquad
\alpha_3=9/20,\qquad \beta=3/10
\]
has objective \(291/100\) and \(\psi_0=\psi_1=\psi_2=0\). The first low constraint is slack, while the second low, third low, and final-high constraints are active. This supports an active-pool interpretation for lows \(0.3,0.4\), but does not prove a scalar adjacent-merge or PAVA rule.

## LB-047: Strictly Increasing-Low One-Drought Closed Form

For the one-drought path
\[
h^M,0^{N_0},\ell_1,0^{N_1},\ell_2,\ldots,0^{N_{r-1}},\ell_r,0^L,h,
\qquad 0<\ell_1<\cdots<\ell_r<h,
\]
work in the formal left-limit model and use the notation of LB-044. Set
\[
H=(M-1)_+,\qquad
B_0=N_0+\mathbf 1_{\{M\ge1\}},\qquad
B_i=N_i+1\quad(1\le i\le r-1),
\]
\[
C_j=\sum_{i=0}^{j-1}B_i,\qquad
S_j=[\ell_j(H+C_j)-hH]_+,\qquad S_0=0,\qquad
\Delta_j=S_j-S_{j-1}.
\]
Then
\[
D_n=Z_{\rm inc}:=\max_{1\le k\le r}
\left[
\left(1-\frac{\ell_k}{h}\right)S_k
+\sum_{j>k}\frac{\ell_k(h-\ell_j)}{h\ell_j}\Delta_j
\right].
\]

Dual lower bound. Fix \(k\), put \(\beta=\ell_k/h\), set \(\ell_{r+1}=h\), and define tail weights
\[
A_j=\sum_{q\ge j}\alpha_q=
\begin{cases}
1-\ell_k/h, & j\le k,\\
\ell_k/\ell_j-\ell_k/h, & j>k.
\end{cases}
\]
Then
\[
\alpha_j=0\ (j<k),\qquad
\alpha_k=1-\ell_k/\ell_{k+1},\qquad
\alpha_j=\ell_k(1/\ell_j-1/\ell_{j+1})\ (j>k),
\]
so \(\alpha_j\ge0\), and \(\sum_j\alpha_j+\beta=1\). Strict increase gives \(m_{i,j}=\ell_j\). For a candidate quote level \(\ell_s\), the raw segment pressure is
\[
\ell_s A_{\max\{i+1,s\}}-\beta(h-\ell_s),
\]
which is \(\le0\) in all cases and is \(0\) for \(\ell_k\) when \(i<k\), or for \(\ell_{i+1}\) when \(i\ge k\). Hence every \(\psi_i=0\), and the dual objective is
\[
\sum_j\alpha_jS_j=\sum_jA_j\Delta_j=Z_k.
\]

Primal upper bound. Let \(k\) maximize \(Z_k=z\). The recurrence
\[
Z_{k+1}-Z_k=\frac{\ell_{k+1}-\ell_k}{\ell_k}(Z_k-S_k)
\]
allows choosing a maximizer with \(S_{k-1}\le z\le S_k\). Define
\[
y_k=S_k-z,\qquad y_j=\Delta_j\quad(j>k),
\]
and place total mass \(y_j/\ell_j\) at quote level \(\ell_j^-\). These masses give low-horizon coverage \(S_t-z\) for all \(t\ge k\), and their final-high debt satisfies
\[
\sum_{j\ge k}(h-\ell_j)\frac{y_j}{\ell_j}=z.
\]
They can be scheduled into segments with eligibility \(i<j\) by the nested Hall inequalities
\[
\sum_{j=k}^t\frac{y_j}{\ell_j}\le C_t,\qquad t\ge k.
\]
For \(t\ge k\), this is equivalent to
\[
z\ge S_k+\ell_k\sum_{j=k+1}^t\frac{\Delta_j}{\ell_j}-\ell_k C_t,
\]
which follows from
\[
z-\left(S_k+\ell_k\sum_{j=k+1}^t\frac{\Delta_j}{\ell_j}-\ell_k C_t\right)
=
\frac{\ell_k}{h}(hC_t-S_t)
+\sum_{j>t}\frac{\ell_k(h-\ell_j)}{h\ell_j}\Delta_j\ge0.
\]
Here \(S_t\le hC_t\) and \(\Delta_j\ge0\) are consequences of actual increasing-low path feasibility. The resulting primal has every low and final-high deficit at most \(z\), matching the dual.

Status: accepted local theorem for actual path-feasible strictly increasing lows, conditional on LB-044. In the original strict-crossing action space it is implemented by \(\ell_j-\eta_t\) and \(h-\eta_t\). It should not be applied to duplicate lows, nonmonotone lows, arbitrary reduced-LP data, repeated droughts, or global \(D_n=o(n)\Rightarrow o(T)\) gluing.

